Later on we'll look at lighting tools, environment light mixer,

18
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:07.209
and light mixer, and we're going to do an interactive build

19
00:01:07.209 --> 00:01:11.450
of light for our hero object that I've given to you guys

20
00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.599
and it'll be a lot of fun.

21
00:01:13.599 --> 00:01:17.280
So let's go ahead and take a look at lighting terminology and types

22
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.519
and how that's all working with Unreal.

23
00:01:20.519 --> 00:01:23.799
First up we'll look at direct lighting.

24
00:01:23.799 --> 00:01:24.519
What does that mean?

25
00:01:24.519 --> 00:01:26.519
When it comes to this real time scenario,

26
00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.760
a lot of people can be unfamiliar with Unreal,

27
00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.120
specifically in lighting because real time is very different

28
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.140
than an offline render.

29
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.019
In this particular case, direct lighting is a light

30
00:01:37.019 --> 00:01:39.579
that falls onto the surface without any interference.

31
00:01:39.579 --> 00:01:41.420
The light travels directly to the surface

32
00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.959
and that surface receives the full color spectrum of light.

33
00:01:44.959 --> 00:01:46.719
We also have indirect lighting.

34
00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:48.560
You'll see that many times in the details

35
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.879
for particular lights that you're working with.

36
00:01:50.879 --> 00:01:54.239
It's a lighting that has been reflected by other surfaces nearby

37
00:01:54.319 --> 00:01:56.640
as the light bounces off these surfaces,

38
00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.560
light waves are absorbed or reflected

39
00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.480
based on the surface's properties

40
00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.799
and passed onto the surface you were looking at.

41
00:02:02.799 --> 00:02:05.200
Thus indirect lighting contributes to the mood

42
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.799
and overall light intensity.

43
00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:09.789
We also have shadows.

44
00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:11.469
Now there's several ways to approach shadows,

45
00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.669
but in Unreal there's the bake method

46
00:02:15.389 --> 00:02:18.590
and you'll see in Unreal takes a snapshot of the mesh actor

47
00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:20.750
from light's point of view and projects that information

48
00:02:21.469 --> 00:02:24.030
on another mesh actor on the opposite side.

49
00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.629
In the case of virtual shadow maps,

50
00:02:25.629 --> 00:02:28.909
shadows update in real time using mesh distance fields

51
00:02:28.909 --> 00:02:32.030
for accurate calculations and updates with movable lights.

52
00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:34.719
So static versus dynamic.

53
00:02:34.719 --> 00:02:36.560
What does that exactly mean when it comes Unreal?

54
00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:38.840
Let's break this down.

55
00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.479
We have a brand new system that came through with Unreal 5

56
00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.240
and that is Lumen.

57
00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:45.319
It's our fully dynamic global illumination

58
00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:46.759
and reflection system

59
00:02:46.759 --> 00:02:49.400
that is designed for next generation consoles,

60
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.120
but it is not limited to consoles or games.

61
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.719
It can be used for Final Pixel.

62
00:02:53.719 --> 00:02:57.800
It can be used for VR training or VR experiences

63
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.080
and it can be used for virtual production.

64
00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.159
It is the default global illumination

65
00:03:03.159 --> 00:03:05.400
and reflection system in Unreal currently.

66
00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.759
Lumen renders diffuse,

67
00:03:07.759 --> 00:03:10.080
inter-reflection with infinite bounces

68
00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.000
and indirect specular reflections and large detailed environments

69
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.199
at scales ranging from millimeters to kilometers.

70
00:03:17.199 --> 00:03:19.199
So it's really nicely optimized

71
00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:22.080
so it can scale down nicely to your needs.

72
00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:27.389
Now, baked static lighting and also stationary,

73
00:03:27.389 --> 00:03:30.430
which stationary is used in that particular direction,

74
00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:33.789
is pre-computed rendered lighting.

75
00:03:33.789 --> 00:03:36.110
Baked lighting is created using Unreal's

76
00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:38.110
Lightmass Radiosity Baker.

77
00:03:39.229 --> 00:03:41.229
And you'll see that when the thing called swarm,

78
00:03:41.229 --> 00:03:42.990
you'll see it on the bottom of your screen

79
00:03:43.629 --> 00:03:44.590
or swarm agent,

80
00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.909
which will appear if you are using the baking system.

81
00:03:48.009 --> 00:03:50.650
It'll be on the bottom right hand side typically.

82
00:03:51.479 --> 00:03:53.960
It is similar to rendering lighting with V-Ray

83
00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:55.719
or Corona, et cetera.

84
00:03:55.719 --> 00:03:58.520
It is used to create high quality indirect lighting

85
00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.680
using light and shadow maps.

86
00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.719
Baked lighting cannot be altered at runtime

87
00:04:03.719 --> 00:04:06.360
but has little impact on real-time performance.

88
00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.919
Now, static lighting is your primary baked source.

89
00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.280
Stationary light is used within that scenario.

90
00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:15.400
Stationary light can be used for movable objects,

91
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.399
for objects moving in the environment

92
00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:19.639
and static would not affect those lights.

93
00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.120
So there's a lot of give and take.

94
00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.399
You have to be very strategic

95
00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:27.240
and deterministic when using baked lighting.

96
00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.480
But baked lighting can give you some really cool results

97
00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:30.360
at a cheap cost,

98
00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:34.680
yet not quite as realistic or performance ready

99
00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:38.040
as Lumen when it comes to being able to change things on the fly.

100
00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:41.069
Now, real-time that takes us to our next topic,

101
00:04:41.069 --> 00:04:42.670
real-time dynamic lighting.

102
00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:44.829
It's lighting that is updated each frame.

103
00:04:44.829 --> 00:04:47.310
It is fully dynamic and can be moved around

104
00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:48.990
and modified at runtime.

105
00:04:49.069 --> 00:04:53.389
It has no global illumination component, only direct light.

106
00:04:53.389 --> 00:04:56.910
It is also expensive to use, affects a real-time performance.

107
00:04:58.319 --> 00:05:03.439
Now, dynamic light in the sense that you're outside of Lumen,

108
00:05:03.439 --> 00:05:06.480
say you used a dynamic light in baked mode,

109
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:07.839
you're not going to get the GI

110
00:05:07.839 --> 00:05:09.519
that you're necessarily going for.

111
00:05:10.079 --> 00:05:12.639
But that's where Lumen kind of breaks the mold.

112
00:05:13.629 --> 00:05:16.110
Unreal can use both static and dynamic lighting.

113
00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.470
That's that note here.

114
00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.269
So architectural visualization will do this sometimes,

115
00:05:20.269 --> 00:05:22.110
although now they're using more of Lumen.

116
00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.470
But the projects, the project assets,

117
00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.910
typically stationary in baked static lighting

118
00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:30.910
is mostly used older way.

119
00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:32.269
Now, Lumen can be used,

120
00:05:32.269 --> 00:05:34.910
but there is currently some reflection limitations.

121
00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:36.110
So when you're using Lumen,

122
00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.350
you're going to be leaning more towards fully dynamic.

123
00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:39.709
Real light is movable

124
00:05:39.709 --> 00:05:42.189
and Lumen will give you global illumination.

125
00:05:42.189 --> 00:05:45.310
So that's kind of breaking the old mold.

126
00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:46.750
It's basically the new guard.

127
00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:48.589
It's a new way to do things.

128
00:05:49.389 --> 00:05:51.629
Now, before Lumen, there was ray tracing.

129
00:05:51.629 --> 00:05:53.550
So ray tracing, this type of lighting

130
00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:54.990
is real-time global illumination

131
00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:57.389
updated with all lights set to movable.

132
00:05:57.389 --> 00:06:00.750
If they are animated, the GI updates on the fly

133
00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:02.029
and the same thing goes with Lumen

134
00:06:02.029 --> 00:06:04.189
because Lumen borrows some of that architecture.

135
00:06:04.189 --> 00:06:05.790
Note some aspects of ray tracing

136
00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:07.149
is taken into account with Lumen

137
00:06:07.149 --> 00:06:08.430
as you see in the bottom here,

138
00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:10.670
such as reflections and some light calculations.

139
00:06:11.579 --> 00:06:15.740
So each one of these has a very specific mindset

140
00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:16.860
when you start diving into it.

141
00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:18.220
Now, we're not going to get into ray trace

142
00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:19.500
for this first course,

143
00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.740
but we will talk about Lumen specifically.

144
00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:23.339
We're not going to get into baking

145
00:06:23.339 --> 00:06:25.180
and how to work with a baking scenario.

146
00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:26.699
That is for the other classes,

147
00:06:27.339 --> 00:06:29.500
more advanced classes coming down the pipe.

148
00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:30.459
But for the most part,

149
00:06:30.459 --> 00:06:32.379
we're going to be focusing more on Lumen.

150
00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:35.839
So the types of lights and how to use them.

151
00:06:36.699 --> 00:06:38.699
We're going to get more into the properties

152
00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:40.860
and how to edit them.

153
00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.220
But for this particular section,

154
00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:44.139
we're going to just give you an overview

155
00:06:44.139 --> 00:06:44.860
on how these work.

156
00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:46.379
So directional light.

157
00:06:47.019 --> 00:06:47.660
Awesome.

158
00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:49.660
It works just like a regular directional light

159
00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:51.500
you would have with Maya or 3D Studio Max

160
00:06:52.139 --> 00:06:54.459
or many other 3D packages.

161
00:06:54.459 --> 00:06:55.899
It is your primary sunlight.

162
00:06:56.879 --> 00:06:58.480
There's also the point light.

163
00:06:58.480 --> 00:06:59.920
Notice I put Omni here.

164
00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.639
Well, if you're more of a 3D Studio Max person,

165
00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:03.920
you'll get that point.

166
00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.160
It works kind of like a bulb or a mini sunlight

167
00:07:06.879 --> 00:07:08.480
and it can be used definitely

168
00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:09.839
for particular scenarios.

169
00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:11.899
There's the spotlight,

170
00:07:11.899 --> 00:07:13.740
which is your typical spotlight with Unreal.

171
00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.060
Has that per number and has a cone angle.

172
00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:17.339
All those things can be controlled.

173
00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:19.680
We also have the rec light.

174
00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:20.879
The rec light is great.

175
00:07:20.879 --> 00:07:22.480
Now you hear me repeat this several times.

176
00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:24.560
The rec light is like a series of point lights

177
00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:27.199
within a square that can be shaped into a rectangle

178
00:07:27.839 --> 00:07:29.279
and many shapes and forms.

179
00:07:29.279 --> 00:07:30.240
We'll get into that later.

180
00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:33.519
It is one of my favorite ones for fill light.

181
00:07:33.519 --> 00:07:34.800
It works fantastic.

182
00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:37.560
Then we also have the skylight.

183
00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:41.759
Now the skylight is used many times

184
00:07:41.759 --> 00:07:43.120
with architectural rendering.

185
00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:44.480
They'll pull into the scene

186
00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:45.519
and I like to use it too

187
00:07:45.519 --> 00:07:48.160
because it gives me a nice little GI bounce everywhere.

188
00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:51.519
Your skylight can also be attached to an HDR image

189
00:07:51.519 --> 00:07:52.079
if you want to.

190
00:07:53.199 --> 00:07:56.160
Otherwise it will use the information,

191
00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:58.720
the data and the scene to bounce your light around

192
00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.199
but you can load an HDR image

193
00:08:01.199 --> 00:08:04.079
such as ones you can get at HDR Haven.

194
00:08:04.079 --> 00:08:05.120
They're working really great

195
00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:07.040
and those are open source.

196
00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:09.199
There's also HDR backdrop

197
00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:12.399
which is similar to the skylight

198
00:08:12.399 --> 00:08:14.240
but in the fact that it is a backdrop

199
00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.920
where you can actually see the image

200
00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.399
but it will also bounce the light around

201
00:08:18.399 --> 00:08:19.839
like you would with a skylight.

202
00:08:19.839 --> 00:08:22.480
So it's using the HDRI backdrop.

203
00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:24.800
It's a predefined HDR.

204
00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.360
Now this one typically is used a lot of times for product

205
00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:32.240
and for vehicles.

206
00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.039
It's not necessarily used for in a game scenario

207
00:08:35.759 --> 00:08:37.600
and if it's used for virtual production

208
00:08:38.639 --> 00:08:40.000
and you just need something down and dirty

209
00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.240
it might be perfect in some scenarios

210
00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:47.360
and but not really for final pixel or for video games

211
00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:49.360
and the fact that if you turn your camera

212
00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.519
you can kind of see that it's kind of fake

213
00:08:51.519 --> 00:08:52.799
but it works really great

214
00:08:52.799 --> 00:08:54.559
has a lighting blueprint set up for it

215
00:08:55.279 --> 00:08:59.360
and it may be great for what you want to do, may not

216
00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:00.480
but if you want realism

217
00:09:01.039 --> 00:09:04.320
you might want to steer more towards directional light

218
00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:06.080
skylight and a few of the other lights

219
00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:07.120
depending on your scene

220
00:09:07.759 --> 00:09:08.960
but it's really great.

221
00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:11.919
A lot of our product people use them

222
00:09:11.919 --> 00:09:13.679
our clients such as automotive

223
00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:16.639
and architectural

224
00:09:17.549 --> 00:09:19.549
and the next we have sun in sky

225
00:09:19.549 --> 00:09:21.149
so this lighting blueprint

226
00:09:21.149 --> 00:09:23.309
allows you to pull this into the scene

227
00:09:23.309 --> 00:09:25.549
and makes it so that you can change the time of day

228
00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:28.350
using longitude and latitude

229
00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:30.990
and we're not going to get really deep into these last two

230
00:09:31.549 --> 00:09:33.629
these were covered in some other classes

231
00:09:33.629 --> 00:09:35.230
but for the most part we're going to be covering

232
00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:38.509
skylight, rec light, spotlight, point light, and directional light.

233
00:09:39.659 --> 00:09:41.419
The sun in sky is great

234
00:09:41.419 --> 00:09:43.179
because you can actually drag it into a scene

235
00:09:43.179 --> 00:09:44.220
and then you can punch

236
00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:45.259
again like I said

237
00:09:45.259 --> 00:09:47.419
you can punch in your exact time of day that you need

238
00:09:47.419 --> 00:09:50.460
longitude, latitude, time of day, and so forth.

239
00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:52.860
Now it's not quite as robust as you would get

240
00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:56.539
as I think 3D Studio Max is one

241
00:09:56.539 --> 00:09:58.860
and I think Maya also has one

242
00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:01.179
with the longitude and latitude

243
00:10:01.179 --> 00:10:03.019
Max is a little bit more robust I believe

244
00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:05.659
but it gets pretty close.

245
00:10:06.519 --> 00:10:08.120
That's it with this course

246
00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:09.399
and you can see we have a link here

247
00:10:09.399 --> 00:10:10.759
if you want to dive in a little deeper

248
00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:13.000
in the next course we're going to look at

249
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.360
lighting properties and settings.

250
00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:16.360
Thanks again.
